\title{Time-Travel Logic: A Fixed-Point Theory of Causal Consistency}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained mathematical theory of closed timelike curves (CTCs)
and time-travel paradoxes, organized around a single object: the \emph{loop map}
$f \colon S \to S$, which records the net effect on the state of the world of one
traversal of a closed timelike curve. We identify the Novikov self-consistency
principle with the existence of a fixed point of $f$, and prove this abstract
condition equivalent to the existence of a concrete \emph{closed timelike
history} of the discrete loop. We formalize the grandfather paradox as a
fixed-point-free (``paradoxical'') loop and prove it genuinely inconsistent.
Three positive consistency guarantees follow from classical fixed-point theorems:
monotone loops on a complete lattice are self-consistent (Knaster--Tarski);
continuous loops on the phase interval $[0,1]$ are self-consistent (the
one-dimensional Brouwer property, via the intermediate value theorem), a toy
model of the conjecture that every CTC in the G\"odel universe is
self-consistent; and involutive loops on a state space of odd cardinality are
self-consistent (a parity argument). Finally, we develop the many-worlds
resolution: a branching evolution on $S \times \mathbb{N}$ in which the traveler
is always sent to a fresh branch. We prove that branching never repeats a state,
and that \emph{every} paradoxical action nonetheless admits a fully consistent
branching history. The grandfather action, impossible in a single timeline,
becomes consistent in the multiverse.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Time travel into the past raises the possibility of \emph{causal loops}:
sequences of events $e_1 \to e_2 \to \dots \to e_n \to e_1$ in which each event
causes the next and the last causes the first. Such a loop, a \emph{closed
timelike curve} (CTC), is not forbidden by the field equations of general
relativity---explicit solutions such as the G\"odel universe contain them---and
it forces a foundational question: which causal loops describe a coherent
history, and which collapse into paradox?

The physics literature answers with the \emph{Novikov self-consistency
principle}: the only processes that can occur around a CTC are those globally
consistent with themselves. The grandfather paradox---a traveler who prevents
their own existence---is then excluded not by fiat but because no self-consistent
history realizes it. This paper renders that informal principle as mathematics.

Our organizing abstraction is deliberately minimal. Whatever the underlying
spacetime, the net effect of traversing a CTC once is a function
$f \colon S \to S$ on the space $S$ of world-states. We call $f$ the \emph{loop
map}. The self-consistency principle then reads: the loop admits a consistent
history if and only if $f$ has a fixed point. This reframing converts a
philosophical debate into a question in fixed-point theory.

\section{The loop map and the self-consistency principle}

Fix a type $S$ of \emph{world-states}---an exhaustive description of everything
the loop can affect.

\begin{definition}[causal loop]
A \emph{causal loop} (closed timelike curve) on $S$ is a function
$f \colon S \to S$, called the \emph{loop map} or \emph{evolution}. The value
$f(s)$ is the world-state produced by feeding $s$ once around the loop.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[self-consistency]
A causal loop $f$ is \emph{self-consistent} if there exists a world-state
reproduced by one traversal: $\exists\, s \in S,\ f(s) = s$. Such an $s$ is a
\emph{fixed point} of $f$; it is a history compatible with itself.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[fixed-point criterion]
A causal loop $f$ is self-consistent if and only if $f$ has a fixed point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition: self-consistency is by definition the existence of
$s$ with $f(s) = s$, which is exactly the existence of a fixed point.
\end{proof}

\section{Discrete loops and closed timelike histories}

A CTC is physically a chain of causal steps, not a single jump. We model a loop
of length $n$ by a sequence of step maps
$\text{steps} \colon \mathbb{N} \to (S \to S)$, where $\text{steps}(k)$ maps the
state at event $e_{k+1}$ to the state at event $e_{k+2}$.

\begin{definition}[traversal]
The \emph{partial traversal} is defined by recursion:
\[
  \tr(0, s) = s, \qquad \tr(k+1, s) = \text{steps}(k)\big(\tr(k, s)\big).
\]
The full length-$n$ loop map is $s \mapsto \tr(n, s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[closed timelike history]
A \emph{closed timelike history} of the length-$n$ loop is a labeling
$h \colon \mathbb{N} \to S$ such that $h(k+1) = \text{steps}(k)(h(k))$ for all
$k < n$ (steps realized) and $h(n) = h(0)$ (loop closes).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[traversal recovers a history]
If $h$ is a closed timelike history of the length-$n$ loop, then
$\tr(k, h(0)) = h(k)$ for all $k \le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. The base case is $\tr(0, h(0)) = h(0)$. For the step, assume
$\tr(k, h(0)) = h(k)$ with $k < n$. Then $\tr(k+1, h(0)) =
\text{steps}(k)(\tr(k, h(0))) = \text{steps}(k)(h(k)) = h(k+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Novikov equivalence]
The length-$n$ loop is self-consistent---its loop map $s \mapsto \tr(n, s)$ has a
fixed point---if and only if it admits a closed timelike history:
\[
  \big(\exists\, s,\ \tr(n, s) = s\big) \iff \big(\exists\, h,\ h \text{ is a
  closed timelike history of length } n\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Given $s$ with $\tr(n,s)=s$, define $h(k) = \tr(k, s)$. The
step-realized condition holds by definition of $\tr$, and $h(n) = \tr(n, s) = s =
h(0)$. ($\Leftarrow$) Given a closed timelike history $h$, take $s = h(0)$. By
the lemma with $k = n$, $\tr(n, h(0)) = h(n) = h(0)$, so $h(0)$ is a fixed point.
\end{proof}

\section{The grandfather paradox}

\begin{definition}[paradoxical loop]
A loop map $f \colon S \to S$ is \emph{paradoxical} if $f(s) \neq s$ for all
$s \in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[paradoxical loops are inconsistent]
A paradoxical loop map has no fixed point, hence is not self-consistent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A fixed point $s$ would satisfy $f(s) = s$, contradicting $f(s) \neq s$.
\end{proof}

We instantiate this with the grandfather action. Reduce the ancestor's fate to a
single bit, $S = \{\text{alive}, \text{dead}\}$, identified with the boolean
values. The traveler's action flips the bit: the loop map is boolean negation
$\lnot$.

\begin{proposition}[the grandfather loop is paradoxical]
Boolean negation $\lnot$ is paradoxical: $\lnot s \neq s$ for both values of $s$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\lnot\,\text{true} = \text{false} \neq \text{true}$ and $\lnot\,\text{false} =
\text{true} \neq \text{false}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[the grandfather paradox is impossible]
The grandfather loop $\lnot$ admits no self-consistent history.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the previous proposition with the theorem that paradoxical loops are
inconsistent.
\end{proof}

\section{Positive consistency guarantees}

\subsection{Order: monotone loops (Knaster--Tarski)}

\begin{theorem}[monotone loops are self-consistent]
Let $S$ be a complete lattice and $f \colon S \to S$ monotone. Then $f$ is
self-consistent; its least fixed point $\lfp(f) = \bigsqcap \{ x : f(x) \le x \}$
satisfies $f(\lfp(f)) = \lfp(f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
This is the Knaster--Tarski theorem. Let $P = \{x : f(x) \le x\}$ and
$m = \bigsqcap P$. For $x \in P$, monotonicity gives $f(m) \le f(x) \le x$;
taking the infimum, $f(m) \le m$, so $m \in P$. Applying $f$, $f(f(m)) \le f(m)$,
so $f(m) \in P$ and $m \le f(m)$. Hence $f(m) = m$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Topology: continuous loops on a phase interval}

\begin{theorem}[continuous loops are self-consistent]
Let $f \colon [0,1] \to [0,1]$ be continuous. Then there exists $s \in [0,1]$
with $f(s) = s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The one-dimensional Brouwer property, via the intermediate value theorem. Put
$g(x) = f(x) - x$, continuous on $[0,1]$. Since $f$ maps into $[0,1]$,
$g(0) = f(0) \ge 0$ and $g(1) = f(1) - 1 \le 0$. A continuous function
nonnegative at $0$ and nonpositive at $1$ vanishes at some $s \in [0,1]$; there
$f(s) = s$.
\end{proof}

This is a toy model of the conjecture that every closed timelike curve in the
G\"odel universe is self-consistent.

\subsection{Parity: involutive loops on odd state spaces}

\begin{theorem}[odd involutive loops are self-consistent]
Let $S$ be finite of odd cardinality and $f \colon S \to S$ an involution
($f(f(s)) = s$). Then $f$ has a fixed point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
As an involution, $f$ is a permutation whose cycles have length $1$ or $2$. The
length-$2$ cycles partition the non-fixed states into disjoint pairs
$\{s, f(s)\}$, an even number of states. If $|S|$ is odd, not all states can be
so paired, so at least one is fixed.
\end{proof}

\section{Branching (many-worlds) time travel}

The many-worlds interpretation resolves paradox by sending the traveler to a
\emph{fresh branch} rather than forcing the loop to close. We model the
multiverse state as a pair $(s, b) \in S \times \mathbb{N}$.

\begin{definition}[branching evolution]
For an action $a \colon S \to S$, the \emph{branching evolution} is
$\branch_a \colon S \times \mathbb{N} \to S \times \mathbb{N}$,
$\branch_a(s, b) = (a(s), b + 1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[a fresh branch every time]
For any $a$, the branching evolution $\branch_a$ is paradoxical as a loop map on
$S \times \mathbb{N}$: it has no fixed point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A fixed point $(s,b)$ would force $b + 1 = b$, impossible in $\mathbb{N}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[branching history exists]
For any $a$ and any $s_0$, there is a history
$H \colon \mathbb{N} \to S \times \mathbb{N}$ with $H(0) = (s_0, 0)$ and
$H(k+1) = \branch_a(H(k))$ for all $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $H$ by recursion: $H(0) = (s_0, 0)$, $H(k+1) = \branch_a(H(k))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[branching resolves every paradox]
Let $a \colon S \to S$ be any paradoxical action. Then (1) there is no $s$ with
$a(s) = s$; and (2) the branching model admits a fully consistent branching
history with $H(0) = (s_0, 0)$ and $H(k+1) = \branch_a(H(k))$ for all $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Part (1) is the definition of paradoxical. Part (2) is the previous theorem,
which places no hypothesis on $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[grandfather in the multiverse]
The grandfather action $\lnot$ has no single-timeline fixed point, yet admits a
consistent branching history from any $s_0$: the traveler kills the ancestor in a
new branch and lives on there.
\end{corollary}

\section{Applications and future directions}

The theory yields a decision procedure for consistency of any modeled loop: form
the loop map $f$ and ask whether it has a fixed point. Impossibility comes from
fixed-point-free structure; guaranteed consistency comes from order, topology, or
parity; and branching resolves the remainder. Loop maps are ubiquitous---in
economic equilibria, population recurrences, numerical fixed-point iteration, and
feedback in control and programming semantics---so the dichotomy transfers
directly beyond time machines.

Natural next steps include: a full Brouwer fixed-point treatment for
higher-dimensional CTC phase spaces; an honest formalization of the G\"odel
metric so that its CTCs' consistency becomes a corollary; a quantitative Novikov
principle measuring the fixed-point set; a categorical/tree model of the
branching multiverse proving acyclicity in full generality; and a combinatorial
classification of self-consistent finite loops by cycle structure.

\section{Conclusion}

Modeling a closed timelike curve by its loop map $f \colon S \to S$ turns the
Novikov self-consistency principle into the existence of a fixed point, and
equates it with the existence of a closed timelike history. Within this frame the
grandfather paradox is provably impossible, three classical fixed-point theorems
supply broad guarantees of consistency, and a branching multiverse resolves every
paradoxical action. The logic of time travel is the logic of fixed points.

\end{document}

